   $cv13${"title":"Self-leadership, confidence and wellbeing","description":"Know your strengths and values, start your personal brand, set a four-week goal and build habits that keep you well and confident at work.","objectives":["Describe at least three personal strengths, two interests and two values, with an example of each from real life","Write a first-draft personal brand statement (two sentences) based on strengths, values and the help they can offer others","Set one SMART four-week learning goal and name the first step they will take this week","Practise three confident workplace behaviours (introducing yourself, asking for help, accepting feedback)","Identify two wellbeing habits and one support route they can use during the programme","Explain the programme expectations, the learning agreement and how to raise a safeguarding concern"],"activities":["Welcome and learning agreement","Icebreaker - two truths and a strength","Values, confidence and self-leadership mini-workshop","Strengths map and personal brand statement","Confidence at work - practice rounds","Wellbeing and resilience","SMART goal and accountability partner","Reflection and exit ticket"],"expected_output":"Completed strengths map (strengths, interests, values, help I can offer); First-draft personal brand statement (two sentences); Goal card with a SMART four-week goal, first step and two wellbeing habits; Accountability partner agreed; Baseline self-assessment and exit ticket submitted","assessment_notes":"DRAFT (V13.2 curriculum revision, 19 Nov 2026): full lesson plan with timed segments, activities, assessment and facilitator notes. Assessment: 5 questions or tasks, checked against the expected outputs. Needs approval by programme management."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.2","purpose":"Learners start the programme knowing what they are good at, what they care about and what they want from the next four weeks. They turn this self-knowledge into the first draft of a personal brand statement, practise confident behaviour they will need at work and in the programme, and learn where to get support. Everything later in DIRA LEARN (track projects, the showcase and the 30-day plan) builds on the goal card and personal brand statement made today.","objectives":["Describe at least three personal strengths, two interests and two values, with an example of each from real life","Write a first-draft personal brand statement (two sentences) based on strengths, values and the help they can offer others","Set one SMART four-week learning goal and name the first step they will take this week","Practise three confident workplace behaviours (introducing yourself, asking for help, accepting feedback)","Identify two wellbeing habits and one support route they can use during the programme","Explain the programme expectations, the learning agreement and how to raise a safeguarding concern"],"topics":[{"title":"Programme orientation and learning agreement","subtopics":["How the four weeks work (core, then one specialist track)","What a good learning space looks like","Respect, consent and privacy in groups and online","How to raise a concern, and the support contacts the programme has formally confirmed"]},{"title":"Strengths, interests and values","subtopics":["Strengths versus skills","Where strengths show up (school, family, church or mosque, chama, part-time work)","Values as decision rules","Spotting strengths in others"]},{"title":"Self-leadership and confidence","subtopics":["Self-leadership - choosing your response","The confidence loop (prepare, act, reflect)","Fixed and growth mindset","Self-talk that helps and self-talk that hurts"]},{"title":"Personal branding (introduction)","subtopics":["What a personal brand is and is not","Strengths plus values plus the help you offer","Consistency between what you say and what you do","First-draft personal brand statement"]},{"title":"Confidence at work","subtopics":["Introducing yourself","Asking for help without apologising","Receiving feedback without defending","Speaking up in a meeting or a WhatsApp group"]},{"title":"Wellbeing and resilience","subtopics":["Sleep, movement, food and screen time","Stress signals","Small recovery habits","Where to get support (friends, family, mentors, DIRA staff, professional help)"]},{"title":"Goal setting and accountability","subtopics":["SMART goals","First steps","Accountability partners","Tracking progress in DIRA LEARN"]}],"segments":[{"minutes":10,"title":"Welcome and learning agreement","kind":"introduction","summary":"Meet the facilitator, see how the four weeks work and agree how the group will learn together."},{"minutes":10,"title":"Icebreaker - two truths and a strength","kind":"group","summary":"Introduce yourself with two true facts and one strength, and guess the strengths of others."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Values, confidence and self-leadership mini-workshop","kind":"lesson","summary":"Learn the difference between strengths, skills and values, and how self-leadership and self-talk shape confidence."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Strengths map and personal brand statement","kind":"exercise","summary":"Map your strengths, interests and values, and turn them into a first-draft personal brand statement."},{"minutes":10,"title":"Confidence at work - practice rounds","kind":"exercise","summary":"Practise introducing yourself, asking for help and receiving feedback in short role-plays."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Wellbeing and resilience","kind":"lesson","summary":"Recognise stress signals, choose small habits that help and know where to get support."},{"minutes":15,"title":"SMART goal and accountability partner","kind":"exercise","summary":"Write one SMART four-week goal with a first step and agree check-ins with an accountability partner."},{"minutes":10,"title":"Reflection and exit ticket","kind":"assessment","summary":"Complete the baseline self-assessment and an exit ticket so the facilitator can see where everyone is starting."}],"guided_exercises":[{"title":"Values cut-down","instructions":"Choose five values from the list, cut to two, and write one moment when each guided a decision."},{"title":"Rewrite your self-talk","instructions":"Write one unhelpful sentence you say to yourself, then rewrite it as a helpful sentence that includes an action."}],"individual_activities":[{"title":"Strengths map","instructions":"Fill the four boxes (Strengths, Interests, Values, Help I can offer) with at least two items each and one real example."},{"title":"Personal brand statement","instructions":"Write a two-sentence statement using the formula. Keep it true, specific and about how you help others."},{"title":"Goal card","instructions":"Write one SMART goal, your first step this week and two wellbeing habits."}],"group_activities":[{"title":"Two truths and a strength","instructions":"In groups of four, share two true facts and one strength; others spot an extra strength in your story."},{"title":"Confidence practice rounds","instructions":"In pairs, practise introducing yourself, asking for help and receiving feedback in a work scenario."}],"examples":["A young woman in Kisumu who sells second-hand clothes online shows strengths in negotiation and photography that later become her personal brand as a reliable mitumba curator.","A boda boda rider in Thika who organises his stage's welfare contributions shows leadership and trust, strengths an employer in logistics would value.","A church youth leader in Eldoret who runs the WhatsApp group for 200 members shows communication and moderation skills.","A learner preparing for an interview at a Nairobi supermarket chain uses the three confidence phrases on her first day as a merchandiser."],"tools":["Slides or flip chart with the programme map and the learning agreement","Strengths map and goal card templates (printed or as a shared document)","List of 20 values (printed or on a slide)","Baseline self-assessment (DIRA LEARN or paper)","Timer"],"assessment":[{"question":"Name three of your strengths and give a real example of one of them."},{"question":"What is the difference between a skill, a strength and a value?"},{"question":"Read your personal brand statement. Who do you help, and how?"},{"question":"Is your four-week goal SMART? Which part is the measurable part?"},{"question":"Where can you go for support during the programme if you are struggling or feel unsafe?"}],"expected_outputs":["Completed strengths map (strengths, interests, values, help I can offer)","First-draft personal brand statement (two sentences)","Goal card with a SMART four-week goal, first step and two wellbeing habits","Accountability partner agreed","Baseline self-assessment and exit ticket submitted"],"homework":["Show your personal brand statement to one person who knows you well and ask whether it sounds like you. Improve it.","Take your first step towards your four-week goal and message your accountability partner by the end of the week.","If you have social media or LinkedIn, look at your profile and note one thing that does not match your personal brand statement (you will fix it in session 2)."],"cross_track_notes":["The personal brand statement made here is reused in session 2 (online profile) and in every track's session 8 showcase, where learners pitch themselves and their project."]}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.2","segments":[{"title":"Welcome and learning agreement","steps":["Welcome learners by name as they join. Start on time even if some are late; latecomers will catch up in the icebreaker.","Show the programme map on one slide: Week 1 shared core (sessions 1 and 2), Weeks 2 to 4 specialist track (sessions 3 to 8), session 8 is the showcase. Say: everything you make in this programme goes into a portfolio you can show an employer, a client or a funder.","Build the learning agreement with the group. Ask: what do you need from each other to learn well? Write their answers. Make sure these five appear: respect different views, one person speaks at a time, cameras or attention on, what is shared personally stays in the room, phones on silent unless used for an activity.","Explain safeguarding in plain words. If anyone, inside or outside the programme, makes you feel unsafe, tell the facilitator or use the support option in DIRA LEARN. Share only the support contacts that programme management has formally confirmed for this cohort (from the facilitator briefing). If a Safeguarding Lead or Deputy has been formally appointed and confirmed, name them; if not, do not name anyone or suggest that someone holds the role - say that concerns go to the facilitator or the DIRA LEARN support option and are passed to programme management. Do not promise secrecy; say concerns are shared only with the people who need to act on them.","Ask everyone to type or say 'I agree' to the learning agreement. Save it and post it in the cohort space after the session."]},{"title":"Icebreaker - two truths and a strength","steps":["Model it first: 'I am Wanjiru from Nakuru, I sell second-hand shoes on weekends, and one strength I have is patience with difficult customers.'","In breakout groups of four (or around the room), each learner gives their name, where they are from, two true facts and one strength. Give 60 seconds each.","Group members name one more strength they heard in the story (for example, the shoe seller also shows planning and negotiation).","Bring the group back. Ask three volunteers to share a strength someone else spotted in them. Point out that others often see strengths we overlook."]},{"title":"Values, confidence and self-leadership mini-workshop","steps":["Explain three words. A skill is something you learned to do (typing, cooking, editing video). A strength is something you do well and that energises you (listening, organising, making people laugh). A value is something that matters to you and guides decisions (honesty, family, faith, independence, community).","Values exercise (5 minutes). Show a list of 20 values. Learners choose five, then cut to two. Ask: when did one of these two values help you make a hard decision? Two volunteers share.","Teach self-leadership: you cannot control everything that happens, but you can choose your response. Use the example of a learner whose phone data runs out before a session: blame and miss it, or plan ahead (buy a bundle early, download materials, tell the facilitator).","Teach the confidence loop: prepare, act, reflect. Confidence comes after action, not before it. Each small action you reflect on makes the next one easier.","Self-talk. Read two sentences: 'I always mess up presentations' and 'My last presentation was nervous, so this time I will practise twice.' Ask which one helps. Learners rewrite one unhelpful sentence they say to themselves.","Explain growth mindset in one sentence - abilities grow with practice and feedback. Connect it to the programme - you are not expected to be good at digital marketing, AI, content or business yet; you are expected to practise."]},{"title":"Strengths map and personal brand statement","steps":["Hand out or share the strengths map template: four boxes - Strengths, Interests, Values, Help I can offer others. Learners fill each box with at least two items and one real example (8 minutes).","Explain personal branding. Your personal brand is what people say about you when you are not in the room. It is built from what you are good at, what you stand for and how you help others, shown consistently online and offline. It is not pretending or showing off.","Give the formula: I am a [strength] person who cares about [value]. I help [who] to [what] by [how]. Example: 'I am an organised and patient person who cares about honesty. I help small shops in my estate to keep customers coming back by answering messages quickly and politely.'","Learners write their own two-sentence statement (5 minutes). Circulate and help anyone stuck by asking what friends usually ask them for help with.","Three volunteers read their statements. Give one specific compliment and one improvement each (for example, make the 'who' more specific).","Tell learners they will improve this statement in session 2 (online profile) and use it in the session 8 showcase."]},{"title":"Confidence at work - practice rounds","steps":["Demonstrate three phrases. Introducing: 'Good morning, I am Brian, I am new in the team and I will be supporting customer orders.' Asking for help: 'I have tried X and Y, could you show me how you would do Z?' Receiving feedback: 'Thank you, can you give me an example so I can improve it?'","In pairs, learners take turns using each phrase in a work scenario (a first day at a supermarket, an internship at a county office, a client call for a small design job). Two minutes per person.","Ask the group which phrase felt hardest and why. Note that asking for help after trying something shows initiative, not weakness."]},{"title":"Wellbeing and resilience","steps":["Ask learners to name signs of stress they notice in themselves (poor sleep, irritability, scrolling for hours, headaches, avoiding people). Write them down without judgement.","Teach four basics: sleep (a regular time helps more than long weekend sleep), movement (a 10-minute walk counts), food and water, and screen boundaries (no phone for the first 30 minutes after waking, mute busy groups).","Teach one recovery tool: the 4-4-6 breath (breathe in for 4, hold for 4, out for 6, repeat three times). Practise it together once.","Support routes: a trusted friend or family member, a mentor, the facilitator, DIRA staff through the support option, and professional help. Say clearly that if anyone feels unsafe or is thinking of harming themselves, they should tell someone immediately and contact the emergency services or a crisis line; the facilitator will share the programme's approved contacts.","Each learner writes two wellbeing habits they will keep during the four weeks on their goal card."]},{"title":"SMART goal and accountability partner","steps":["Explain SMART - Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, Time-bound. Weak goal: 'learn marketing'. SMART goal: 'By the end of week 4 I will create and present a basic marketing campaign for my aunt's cereals shop and get feedback from two real customers.'","Learners write their goal and their first step for this week on the goal card (5 minutes).","Pair learners as accountability partners (from different tracks if possible). Partners read each other's goals and make one suggestion to make it more measurable.","Partners agree how they will check in (for example a WhatsApp message every Sunday evening). Remind them to keep messages respectful and on topic, and that they can tell the facilitator if a partnership is not working."]},{"title":"Reflection and exit ticket","steps":["Learners complete the baseline self-assessment in DIRA LEARN or on paper (confidence 1 to 5 in communication, digital skills, teamwork and goal setting).","Exit ticket - three sentences: one thing I learned about myself today, one thing I will do before session 2, one question I still have.","Close: thank learners, remind them of the session 2 date and time from the timetable, and ask them to bring a phone or computer with access to their social media or LinkedIn account if they have one."]}],"demonstrations":[{"title":"Modelling a personal brand statement","steps":["Write your own strengths map live (keep it honest and simple).","Turn it into a statement using the formula, thinking aloud about each choice.","Show a weak version ('I am hardworking and passionate') next to the improved one, and explain why specific beats general."]},{"title":"Confidence phrases","steps":["Act out a poor example first (mumbling an introduction, apologising for asking a question), then the confident version.","Ask learners what changed (eye contact, clear name and role, specific question)."]}],"assessment_guides":[{"question":"Name three of your strengths and give a real example of one of them.","guide":"Look for strengths (not just skills) with a concrete example. Prompt with 'when did you do that?' if the example is vague."},{"question":"What is the difference between a skill, a strength and a value?","guide":"Skill is learned ability; strength is what you do well and that energises you; value is what matters and guides decisions."},{"question":"Read your personal brand statement. Who do you help, and how?","guide":"The statement should name a specific group of people and a specific way of helping. 'Everyone' or 'many things' needs narrowing."},{"question":"Is your four-week goal SMART? Which part is the measurable part?","guide":"There should be a number, a deliverable or a clear observable result and a date (end of week 4)."},{"question":"Where can you go for support during the programme if you are struggling or feel unsafe?","guide":"Any of the facilitator, the DIRA LEARN support option, a confirmed support contact shared in the session (including a formally appointed Safeguarding Lead or Deputy, if one was named), a mentor, a trusted adult, or emergency services for immediate danger."}],"facilitator":{"preparation":["Check the DIRA LEARN cohort list and attendance register before the session; mark attendance in DIRA LEARN within the session.","Prepare the programme map slide, the values list, the strengths map and goal card templates.","Before the session, get from programme management the support contacts formally confirmed for this cohort, and whether a Safeguarding Lead and Deputy have been formally appointed. Share only confirmed contacts and appointments. Never invent a contact or name someone who has not been formally appointed.","Write your own strengths map and personal brand statement in advance so you can model them."],"delivery":["Keep the energy high in the first 20 minutes; this session sets the tone for the whole programme.","Use learners' names often. Praise effort and specifics, not just talent.","Watch the time on the strengths map; learners who are stuck often need one question ('what do friends ask you for help with?')."],"inclusion_and_safeguarding":["Values and wellbeing discussions can bring up personal difficulties. Never force anyone to share; allow 'pass'.","If a learner discloses harm or risk, do not investigate. Listen, reassure, explain who you must tell, and record and escalate it through the DIRA LEARN safeguarding process the same day (immediately if urgent).","Learners with low data or older phones can complete templates on paper and photograph them later."],"after_session":["Post the learning agreement in the cohort space.","Check exit tickets and answer common questions at the start of session 2.","Note any learner who seemed withdrawn or did not participate and check in privately."]}}$cv13$::jsonb),
